=== Introduction
--- Genesis Chapter 25 [title]
overline: Young Adult Sunday School • Genesis Series
heading: Genesis 25: Esau and Jacob
sub: Abraham Gathered to His People • The Elder Shall Serve the Younger • The Birthright • Spiritual Edom
refs: Genesis 25:1-34
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== The death of Abraham
--- Keturah
kicker: vv. 1–4
sub: Then Again Abraham Took a Wife
scripture: Genesis 25:1-4 | mark: Then again Abraham took a wife, and her name was Keturah
p: Abraham was 175 when he died. He was 137 when Sarah died, and here it says he remarried and had at least 6 sons with Keturah, and they begat many sons, but the physical inheritance and the spiritual blessing, given Abraham by God concerning Jesus, Abraham passes down to Isaac. The child of faith.

--- All That He Had unto Isaac
kicker: vv. 5–6
sub: Gifts to the Sons of the Concubines
scripture: Genesis 25:5-6 | mark: And Abraham gave all that he had unto Isaac

--- Gathered to His People
kicker: vv. 7–8
sub: An Hundred Threescore and Fifteen Years
scripture: Genesis 25:7-8 | mark: and was gathered to his people
p: Abraham died and was gathered to his people, in heaven. It couldn't be into his own "bosom" after all. But the patriarch is given an end of days and an account of his death, which the Bible uses to signify the humanity of the fathers and the prophets.

--- The Cave of Machpelah
kicker: vv. 9–11
sub: God Blessed His Son Isaac
scripture: Genesis 25:9-11 | mark: his sons Isaac and Ishmael buried him

--- Abraham Rejoiced to See My Day
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 25:8
fig: John 8:56 | John 8:56
fig: John 8:58 | John 8:58
callout: Christ Jesus comments on the faith of Abraham and his joy at the fulfillment of the promise made to him in **John 8**. And so it was that Abraham's seed was a blessing to the whole world, especially of those who believe.

--- Abraham's Seed
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 25:5
fig: Galatians 3:26-27 | Galatians 3:26-27
fig: Galatians 3:28-29 | Galatians 3:28-29
callout: Let this comfort you who believe, and drive to envy those of you still on the fence, trusting your own righteousness to provide your own salvation, bringing you to repent. Knowing Abraham trusted God and obtained the righteousness of Christ, and so too do we. For God is not slack concerning His promises. And we who are of Abraham's seed in Christ Jesus are joint heirs with Him. Rejoice!

=== The generations of Ishmael
--- Twelve Princes
kicker: vv. 12–16
sub: The Sons of Ishmael
scripture: Genesis 25:12-16 | mark: twelve princes according to their nations

--- In the Presence of All His Brethren
kicker: vv. 17–18
sub: An Hundred and Thirty and Seven Years
scripture: Genesis 25:17-18

=== Two nations
--- Rebekah His Wife Conceived
kicker: vv. 19–21
sub: Because She Was Barren
scripture: Genesis 25:19-21 | mark: because she was barren: and the LORD was intreated of him
p: Isaac, at 60 years old, finally becomes a father. In an echo of his own birth, Rebekah is as barren as Sarah was. Yet God intervenes, and the couple conceive twin boys.

--- The Elder Shall Serve the Younger
kicker: vv. 22–23
sub: Two Nations Are in Thy Womb
scripture: Genesis 25:22-23 | mark: Two nations are in thy womb; the elder shall serve the younger
p: As God says to Rebekah, there are two nations striving within her. One shall be strong, and the elder will serve the younger.

--- Jacob Have I Loved
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 25:23
fig: Malachi 1:2-3 | Malachi 1:2-3
fig: Romans 9:11-13 | Romans 9:11-13
callout: Now, the Bible says that God hated Esau, and Jacob He loved. This is referring to the nations that come from each, Jacob giving birth to the 12 patriarchs of Israel, Esau the nations of Edomites. Much is made of this distinction, but the fate of Edom is not necessarily reflected in the life of its patriarch, Esau. In the next chapter we will look more closely at that. [[Romans 9:4-15|Read Romans 9:4–15]]

--- Esau and Jacob
kicker: vv. 24–26
sub: His Hand Took Hold on Esau's Heel
scripture: Genesis 25:24-26 | mark: they called his name Esau; his name was called Jacob
p: Esau, the first born, hair red as the pottage he'd sell his birthright for. Red is the meaning of Edom. Jacob, second born, holding on to Esau's foot, as they are taken from their mother.

--- A Plain Man, Dwelling in Tents
kicker: vv. 27–28
sub: A Cunning Hunter, a Man of the Field
scripture: Genesis 25:27-28 | mark: Esau was a cunning hunter; Jacob was a plain man, dwelling in tents
p: God wastes no time on irrelevant details. Isaac's life story is briefly skimmed over. Only the details of the inheritance and blessing matter to the story God is telling. Of the elder serving the younger. Of the law and of faith.
p: And so, we come to adult Esau and Jacob, men established as a hunter, and a man of the field, while the younger tends flocks, living in tents. No great hero and hunter, proficient with weapons like his elder brother, but a plain man.

=== The birthright
--- That Same Red Pottage
kicker: vv. 29–30
sub: For I Am Faint
scripture: Genesis 25:29-30 | mark: Feed me, I pray thee, with that same red pottage; therefore was his name called Edom
p: Jacob makes a red pottage, a lentil soup. Esau, hungry and tired, saying "*Feed me, I pray thee, with that same red pottage; for I am faint*."
p: Most interpret this to just mean normal hunger. Not truly fainting or starving in a serious sense. Just ravenous from being out in the field hunting, and wanting to please his flesh by eating the pottage.

--- Esau Despised His Birthright
kicker: vv. 31–34
sub: Sell Me This Day Thy Birthright
scripture: Genesis 25:31-34 | mark: Sell me this day thy birthright; thus Esau despised his birthright
p: Jacob says he will feed his brother in exchange for his birthright. And despising his birthright, that is not caring about it, willing to trade it for a momentary satisfaction of the flesh, Esau agrees.

--- One Morsel of Meat
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 25:34
fig: Hebrews 12:16-17 | Hebrews 12:16-17
callout: Paul uses this illustration in **Hebrews 12** regarding fornicators among the believers, who have the inheritance but lose the blessings that come with the birthright we share in Christ Jesus for the same momentary satisfaction of the flesh. That later, though he repented of giving away his inheritance by law to his younger brother, having a change of heart too late, and losing the eventual blessings meant for him. [[Hebrews 12:14-17|Read Hebrews 12:14–17]]

--- Like Esau
kicker: Application • The birthright
sub: A Grave Warning
p: Christians who live carnally, after the flesh, despising their birthright received through Christ, losing blessings of God for their own morsel of meat, a simple bowl of pottage, are like Esau. And while we still retain sonship, and gain a share in the kingdom, the blessings we lose here in this life and in the time to come are lost or diminished, regardless of how tearfully we may regret it when our flesh dies. It's a grave warning.
p: We know, having hindsight what a fool Esau, now Edom was. Yet how many times do we do the same, for much less than even a bowl of lentils?

=== Spiritual Edom
--- Faith over Flesh
kicker: Application • The elder and the younger
sub: A Pattern for the Salvation to Come
p: For now, the parallels to Cain and Abel, Sarah and Hagar, Isaac and Ishmael, Jews and Gentiles, even Jesus and Barabbas, the free will choices given to show that God favors faith over the law, spirit over the flesh, blesses them that trust His promises and do His will, giving them the inheritance for their faith, and the casting out of those who are after the flesh, even if by law they have a birthright claim.
p: The entire Old Testament serves as a pattern for the salvation to come offered in Christ. Both the choice to believe, placing faith above flesh, explaining the theology of the New Testament with examples from the Old. And continually we see the eldest by law passed over, to serve the younger, who by faith receives both the birthright and blessing.

--- Not Replacement, Rejection
kicker: Application • Spiritual Edom
sub: They Sold Their Birthright
p: When applied to the Jews and Gentiles, this is called "replacement theology," which is a misnomer. Like Esau, Jews sought an earthly kingdom from an earthly messiah, to satisfy their fleshly desires. They sold their birthright, despising their inheritance, by choosing to kill Christ. By rejecting the blessing that had carried them from Canaan to Egypt, back to the promised land. Through wars, invasions and captivity, they lost everything, for the younger, the Gentiles, to gain.
p: It's not replacement, it's rejection. Spiritual Edom. And like Edom after the flesh, they were made desolate by the Lord.

--- Edom Shall Be a Desolation
kicker: Cross references • Genesis 25:30
fig: Jeremiah 49:10 | Jeremiah 49:10
fig: Jeremiah 49:17-18 | Jeremiah 49:17-18
callout: [[Jeremiah 49:10-18|Read Jeremiah 49:10–18]]

--- A Byword and a Parable
kicker: Application • Spiritual Edom
sub: The Choice Is Made in Man's Heart
p: God didn't "replace" Esau with Jacob. Edom with Israel.
p: And the Jews who killed Jesus and the prophets and persecuted the Gentiles who received the inheritance in faith, are spiritually Edom. The wrath of God abides upon them to the uttermost. They are a byword and a parable. A warning.

--- Older Brothers
kicker: Application • Spiritual Edom
sub: Less than a Bowl of Pottage
p: Modern, dispensationalist Christians who have elevated Jews to coequal status, even giving them honor as "older brothers," claiming that inheritance of the flesh, in the law means they retain the birthright and blessing they rejected when they reject Christ, are carnal and risk going the way of Edom themselves.
p: They so despise their birthright in Christ they are willing to give it up to Jews for a morsel. For less than a bowl of pottage.

--- The Blessings Are Ours
kicker: Application • Spiritual Edom
sub: Too Late
p: They say the "church age" ends in failure. Taken up in a pre-trib rapture to make way for the Jews to receive the Kingdom that is ours in Jesus' name. They question the judgment of God and ignore these warnings found throughout the Bible, in exchange for...what? The false teachers get paid. Hagee and the televangelists and evangelical pastors who promote dispensationalism get filthy lucre, for sure. Vipers being rewarded in the flesh. But the flock, many who are believers, they get nothing of value but the brief and fleeting feeling of approval by a group of people who resent and hate them. "If I bless them I will be blessed!" If you are saved in Christ you've already been blessed! If you live in the spirit, doing the will of the Father, the blessings are ours, in abundance.
p: But for the meaningless approval of man, they sell it all to the wicked, spiritual Edomites.
p: And like Esau, they will see eventually that their choice robbed them of the blessings, and with tears they will repent, but it will be too late.

--- The Choice Is Made in Man's Heart
center: The choice is made in man's heart.
